#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Generate the tetra themes (tetra-dark, tetra-mirage, tetra-light) for
Omarchy and Ghostty.

Tetra is a system: Dark is the definition, and Mirage and Light are Dark placed
on their own ground (background and foreground lightness). See theme_palette().
Dark's source palette() is built like this:

- Shades, orange and brown come from the base24 source scheme in
  tinted-theming/schemes (spec-0.11 branch).
- Bright ANSI colours are the source palette in VARIANTS; normal ones are
  darker versions of them, following the source terminal rule (OKLCH
  lightness offsets).
- Selection is the source selection colour and muted its comment gray.
- Neutral shades (backgrounds, foregrounds, muted) and selection get half of
  the source tint: OKLCH chroma halved, lightness unchanged.

Mirage and Light keep source palettes in VARIANTS too. The showcase page
compares against them; the themes don't use them.

Writes colors.toml, neovim.lua (aether.nvim with Tetra's palette, editor roles
and syntax colours) and icons.theme into
~/.config/omarchy/themes/tetra-<variant>/, and a gradient wallpaper only when
the theme has no backgrounds yet. Also writes the Ghostty theme
~/.config/ghostty/themes/tetra-<variant>, with the same palette as Omarchy's
terminal templates plus the extra shades in 16-21 that ~/.tmux.conf uses (see
~/.config/omarchy/themed/foot.ini.tpl). If the active Omarchy theme is one of
the generated ones, it is re-applied with omarchy-theme-refresh.

Also writes a Paseo theme plugin with all three variants to
~/.config/omarchy/tetra/paseo/. Each daemon reads it from there once installed
with `paseo plugin install`; after regenerating, run `paseo plugin reload tetra`
on each. Paseo's app themes take only eight colours, and its terminal ANSI
colours stay Paseo's own.

Usage: generate [variant...]   (default: all)
"""
